hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2944","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli, narrated to us Hatim ibn Isma'il, narrated to us Ja'far, on the authority of his father, who said:

We entered upon Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) and he mentioned the hadith at length. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) proceeded to the House on the Day of Sacrifice, meaning he went to the Ka'bah. He came to the Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib who were drawing water from Zamzam. He (ﷺ) said: ""Draw water, O Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib! If not for the fear that people would overcome you in your duty of providing water, I would have drawn water with you."" So they handed him a bucket, and he drank from it.

**CHAPTER:**"
"2945","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us 'Asim, on the authority of al-Sha'bi, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) drank from a bucket of Zamzam water while standing.

Abu Bakr (RA) said: He intended to drink from a bucket, not that he drank the entire bucket. This is of the same type that I have informed about in other places in our books, that the name of a thing may apply to part of its components, as in the statement of Allah, the Exalted:

*""And do not be loud in your prayer""* (Surah Al-Isra, 17:110),

where the name ""prayer"" is applied specifically to the recitation. Similarly, the Prophet (ﷺ) said:

*""Allah said: I have divided the prayer between Myself and My servant into two halves,""*

then he mentioned specifically the recitation of Surah Al-Fatiha in the prayer, applying the name ""prayer"" specifically to the recitation of Surah Al-Fatiha in the prayer.

**CHAPTER:**"
